    "body": "<!-- pr-reviewer: 1a6d4017ea6cfa836c7b349d86c2c9a9ac8260d2 -->\n**Needs changes before merge.** There is one small fix: the MCP placement text tells an agent the wrong unit. The placement itself, the refusals and the command flow all held up.\n\n**Requested before merge**\n1. **`crates/app/src/mcp/body_tools.rs`, `s_set_lathe()`: the `tip` schema description gives the wrong unit.** It says the tip is \"in the file's length unit read as mm\". The importer does not read STEP numbers as mm. `geometry/import/step.rs` scales the facets of an inch or metre STEP to millimetres after tessellating (#306, `mm_per_unit`), and the lathe placement is applied to those mm facets. Take an agent driving an inch STEP that follows the schema: it gives the nose centre in the file's inch numbers (say `[1.0, 0.4, 0]`). The tip then lands 25.4x too close to the origin, which can be about 25 mm off on a typical holder. That is inside `MAX_TIP_GAP` (50.8 mm), so nothing refuses it, and the holder is judged in the wrong place. This is the silent-misplacement case the Prime Directive is about.\n   Fix: say \"mm, in the model's frame after import (an inch or metre STEP is scaled to mm; STL and OBJ numbers are read as mm)\". Optionally say the same in the `set_lathe_tool_body` line of `.claude/skills/camcraft-mcp/SKILL.md`. The GUI is already correct, because the Tip fields go through the document unit lens over mm model coordinates.\n\n**What I checked that held**\n- **The frame is the one the law reads.** `SetToolBody::lathe` -> `tool_body_lathe::place` -> `LathePlacement::basis` builds a right-handed basis: x is radial, z is `along` squared onto it, and y = z × x. Any placement is therefore a rotation. A user cannot produce a mirrored holder through the placement; reversing `along` turns the body 180 degrees about the radial direction, which is a different real pose. `vendor_tool_body::a_step_lathe_holder_placed_off_its_model_frame_is_judged_and_its_mirror_stands_clear` pins that the two poses give different verdicts. The preview arrows (amber radial, cyan +Z) and the \"placed: radial a to b, spindle c to d from the tip\" note give the user a way to check the pose before Apply.\n- **Fidelity and size.** Every body-file path now goes through `tool_body_file::read` / `accept`: `from_file`, `lathe_from_file`, `from_step_auto`, `load_aligned` and the MCP tools. A reduced-fidelity STEP (`coarse_mm`) is refused by name, so it is never judged thinner. A file over the facet budget is `ImportError::TooLarge`, not truncated, and an empty mesh is refused by name. I found no remaining `load_model(` on a body path.\n- **Insert zone.** It is still capped by `fits` / `zone_cap` to one insert about the tip (≤ 25.4 mm, a blade's half width along Z), and a zone holding the whole body is refused. A user cannot widen the exemption past one insert box. `zone_of_reaches` passes negative and non-finite reaches through, and `InsertZone::check` names them.\n- **Everything Is a Command.** Apply body queues a single `SetToolBody::lathe` (one history step), and Clear body is `SetToolBody` with `None`. All five MCP writes call the same command constructors and `env.history.execute`. Picks and previews are draft-only. The UI uses app helpers (`model::find_by_name`, `tool_of`) and no raw flecs.\n- **No `unwrap`/`expect`/`panic!`** in the new non-test code. `extent` skips non-finite coordinates.\n- **Process.** `PROCESS_VERSION` 16 -> 17 (main is 16). Decision file `docs/decisions/361-vendor-tool-models.md`. No new crate dependency. Body has a `Changelog:` line and `Refs #361`. No snapshot PNG changes, and the new UI is pinned by kittest AccessKit-label tests (`tool_body_lathe.rs`). New files are all under 400 lines.\n- **Fixture `testdata/models/lathe_holder_offset.step`.** The header reads `FILE_DESCRIPTION('CamCraft test fixture: an offset lathe holder, our own geometry')`, `FILE_NAME(..., (''), (''), 'camcraft', '', '')`, and the length unit is `SI_UNIT(.MILLI.,.METRE.)`. There is no authoring system or vendor name, and it is 245 lines of planar faces.\n\n**Local results (trial merge with origin/main `23c62044`, clean, Windows)**\n| Run | Result |\n|---|---|\n| app `mcp_tool_body` | 5 passed |\n| app `vendor_tool_body` | 3 passed |\n| app `project_roundtrip` | 71 passed |\n| app `tool_body` / `tool_body_bands` / `tool_body_judged` / `tool_body_view` | 12 / 13 / 8 / 7 passed |\n| app `turning_holder` / `turning_insert_zone` (#369 / #396 laws) | 8 / 5 passed |\n| app `holder_library` / `machine_default_holder` / `sim_tool_body` / `tool_csys` / `tool_spun_profile` | 11 / 13 / 7 / 11 / 7 passed |\n| ui `--lib tool_body` (includes the 6 `tool_body_lathe` tests) | 18 passed |\n| ui `--lib structural` | 4 passed |\n| `cargo clippy -p camcraft-app -p camcraft-ui --all-targets -- -D warnings` | clean |\n\n**Questions / notes (not blocking)**\n- **The judge is part and stock only (#1037).** This PR is the first that makes a lathe holder body reachable by a user, and the holder is still not judged against the chuck or the workholding. A green verdict there could be read as \"the holder clears everything\". Consider having the Body section (or the turning op's note) say \"judged against the part and stock only\" until #1037 lands.\n- **STL and OBJ have no unit.** They are read as mm, and the editor's import status does not say so. A one-line \"read as mm\" in the status for those formats would close that gap. The spindle-body path has the same gap; it predates this PR.\n- **For a follow-up issue, not this PR:** `Reach::radii` reads the body at signed tip radius `X` as `|X + x|`. The UI and MCP wording \"+x points away from the spindle axis\" is only true for a tip at X ≥ 0. If any turning path ever carries a tip at negative X (rear turret, or facing past the centre), the body would be read mirrored through the axis.\n- `crates/ui/src/state.rs` is already about 4270 lines and grows by 11 here. That is not blocking.\n\nWhat I did not exercise: a real vendor STEP, inch-STEP placement end to end, clicking picks in the 3D preview, and the GUI itself.\n\nA maintainer will approve and merge by hand.\n",
    "author_association": "OWNER",
